What Problem Does It Solve?

Imagine SAP's cloud platform (BTP) is like a big office building with many departments — a Login desk, an Accounts department, 
a Billing department, a Security office, an HR department. Normally, if you want to check "is everything working today," 
you'd have to walk to every single department, ask them individually, and write down what they said.

This tool is like a single assistant who walks the whole building for you and comes back with one report — "Login desk: working. 
Accounts: locked door. Billing: no access. Security office: all clear." Instead of you doing 8 separate walks, you press one button.

Where consultancies like the ones you're targeting (Incture, msg global, Bosch GCC) actually build this: pre-go-live health checks
before a client's production cutover, recurring quota/cost governance across a client's landscape, and onboarding automation scripts 
that verify a newly provisioned subaccount is correctly configured

Language & Core Libraries:
Tool	What it's for
Python 3.12	-> The programming language everything is written in
requests ->	Makes HTTP calls (the actual "knock on the department's door")
rich ->	Prints colored tables/panels in the terminal instead of plain text
python-dotenv	-> Reads settings from a .env file instead of hardcoding them
PyJWT	-> Decodes the JWT (the digital ID card/access token)

Real SAP APIs Called
XSUAA OAuth — /oauth/token (login)
Accounts Service — /accounts/v1/labels
Entitlements Service — /entitlements/v1/assignments
XSUAA SCIM — /Users
Account Budgets Service — /budgets/v1/budgets
XSUAA Authorization Management API — /sap/rest/authorization/v2/roles and /rolecollections
Audit Log Retrieval API — /auditlog/v2/auditlogrecords


Code Structure (Architecture Pattern)
Layered architecture — three layers that only talk downward, never sideways or upward:
app.py — the menu, controls what happens when you press a number
api/ — talks to SAP directly over the internet (auth, accounts, entitlements, users)
services/ — interprets the data (service key analysis, quota checks, cost checks, reports)
utils/ — shared plumbing (config, logging, dashboard display, error types)
This is the same separation style used in real enterprise software — so no single file becomes a giant tangle of "does everything."
